\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekr1} and QwQ~\cite{qwq} scale inference-time computation through extended chains of thought (CoT). Recent work documents that these models can ``self-jailbreak'': without adversarial input modification, the CoT transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}. We define the first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases as the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP). This failure mode differs qualitatively from adversarial jailbreaks on standard LLMs because the harmful transition originates within the model's own reasoning. Recent work shows that chain-of-thought faithfulness is limited~\citep{chen2025unfaithful}, motivating hidden-state monitoring that bypasses surface text. Before retrospective characterization can inform future detection systems, a prior question must be answered: can current probing methods reliably distinguish pre-CP from post-CP reasoning steps, and under what conditions? This paper provides \emph{retrospective} classification (not online detection) of what hidden-state and text-based probes can and cannot detect; false positive rates remain 11--50\%, so deployment applications are future work.\looseness=-1

Existing safety detectors target fundamentally different problems. Hidden-state (HS) methods such as TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} and SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} classify adversarial inputs at the token level, while text monitors like SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} scan generated reasoning for explicit violations. Neither tracks the gradual, multi-step commitment process. HS probes require choosing which transformer layer to monitor, a researcher degree of freedom typically treated as a minor implementation detail. Before asking which modality is better, a prior methodological question must be answered: how sensitive are probe conclusions to the choice of layer selection metric? We show this choice alone can reverse a study's central conclusion: the same data yield a 14-layer (R1-8B) to 61-layer (QwQ-32B) divergence depending solely on metric choice; for QwQ-32B, at threshold-based layers the HS advantage is +19pp ($p{<}0.005$); at crossing-rate-selected layers, it is non-significant ($p{=}0.83$). Prior hidden-state probing targets standard LLMs~\citep{zhou2024alignment,arditi2024refusal}; prior reasoning safety work remains at the text level~\citep{harmthoughts,safethink}. The intersection---hidden-state monitoring of reasoning model safety trajectories---is unexplored, and existing hidden-state analyses operate across layers (spatial) rather than across generation steps (temporal). \cref{fig:concept_overview} illustrates this metric-induced reversal.\looseness=-1

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/fig_concept_overview.pdf}
\caption{Metric-induced layer selection reversal. The same hidden states, evaluated with different layer selection metrics, yield opposite conclusions about the HS advantage. Crossing-rate composite selects shallow layers (L0--L10), where HS and text probes perform equivalently ($p{=}0.83$); threshold-based metrics select deep layers (L14--L63), where HS probes significantly outperform text (+20pp, $p{<}0.005$). The 14--61 layer gap arises from the metric alone.}
\label{fig:concept_overview}
\end{figure*}

We conduct a systematic analysis across four reasoning models, primarily Qwen-based (3/4 models), with one Llama model providing evidence from a second architecture family, at two scales (7--8B, 32B) on the HarmThoughts benchmark. Our central finding is methodological: the choice of layer selection metric entirely determines whether an HS advantage is observed. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) selects shallow layers for 32B models because shallow representations compress directional information, so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings reflect noise rather than semantic transitions. At these layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage.\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{$n$ (total\,/\,test)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209\,/\,43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228\,/\,47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100\,/\,20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95\,/\,19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Models studied. R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B~\citep{openthinker} is trained with STILL~\citep{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data: HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations (CP = first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases). Split: 60/20/20.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

Threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) share a common confusion matrix and empirically converge; BA independently confirms deep-layer selection (\cref{tab:layer_selection}). At these layers, the HS advantage is graded: \emph{validated via nested 5-fold CV} at 7--8B (precision +20.1 to +21.9pp; \cref{tab:hs_vs_text}), \emph{significant} but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp; \cref{sec:32b-analysis}), and \emph{exploratory} at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation, below MDE; \cref{sec:experiments}).\looseness=-1

Our contributions are threefold. \textbf{(1)~Metric-induced layer selection bias}: CR-composite metrics systematically select shallow layers across all four models, both datasets (HarmThoughts, AdvBench; \cref{app:advbench_layer_bias}), and extend to Gemma-2 (\cref{app:gemma_cross_arch}), producing a qualitative reversal ($p{=}0.83$ vs.\ $p{<}0.005$) from metric choice alone; random-label controls confirm genuine safety labels are required (\cref{sec:layer-results}). While metric sensitivity in probing is well-documented~\citep{belinkov2022probing,voita2020information}, we identify a novel \emph{cascade}---metric choice determines layer, which determines conclusion---whose severity (14--61 layer divergence, qualitative sign reversal) has not, to our knowledge, been documented in the probing literature. \textbf{(2)~Boundary conditions}: the HS advantage is encoder-conditional (diminishes from 4/4 to 1/4 HB with stronger encoders; PCA capacity matching preserves significance) and partially overlaps with position (3/4 linear, 2/4 quadratic; \cref{sec:32b-analysis}). \textbf{(3)~Negative results}: no alternative method matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}); control task selectivity (11--17pp above chance; \cref{app:control}) confirms genuine safety structure. These findings establish that layer selection metric is a critical specification choice in hidden-state safety probing---one severe enough to qualitatively reverse study conclusions.\looseness=-1

