\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass. $^\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$); 32B results are exploratory. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

The HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\dagger$ (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust ($p{=}0.044$, rank 12/12 in HB step-down; 1/5 seeds Bonferroni; \cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds but shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\cref{app:control}). A split-half analysis on OT-7B confirms internal validity (\cref{app:split_half}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d, PCA-reduced to 384d; \cref{app:e5_mistral}) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision). \cref{fig:forest_precision} visualizes the encoder-quality gradient: MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) $\to$ E5-Mistral (2/4) $\to$ BGE-1024d (1/4), with precision narrowing from +15--22pp to +6--18pp. The HS advantage is thus encoder-conditional; layer selection bias (C1) is encoder-independent.\looseness=-1

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_forest_precision.pdf}
\caption{HS precision advantage (pp) over text probes with 95\% bootstrap CI. Blue: vs.\ MiniLM-384d; orange: vs.\ BGE-1024d. Solid: survives HB; hollow: does not. The encoder-quality gradient is monotonic: all 384d comparisons significant; at 1024d only OT-7B survives.}
\label{fig:forest_precision}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 12--20pp of balanced accuracy, but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Under linear residualization, 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (+3.7 to +6.9pp, HB); R1-32B is indistinguishable from zero (+1.9pp, below MDE). Quadratic residualization further absorbs the advantage in 2/4 models (QwQ-32B fully, R1-32B fully; R1-8B and OT-7B retain significance; \cref{tab:position_quadratic}). Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (\cref{sec:temporal}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested CV confirms the 7--8B advantage (10/10 folds, 13--25\% shrinkage; \cref{app:nested_cv}); the OT-7B holdout estimate is an upper bound (nested CV: L21--27 vs.\ holdout L16). R1-32B significance is limited to $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage); QwQ-32B lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps). FPR remains 11--50\%, precluding production deployment (\cref{sec:oracle}).\looseness=-1

\subsection{32B Scale}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

Results at 32B are exploratory$^\dagger$ ($n{=}19$--$20$; MDE 8.6--9.5pp; \cref{app:power_analysis}). Both models show divergent profiles---R1-32B suggests early commitment ($W{=}1$ only) while QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive---but neither meets the MDE threshold for confirmatory conclusions. Full analysis in \cref{app:32b_full}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\cref{app:two_stage}). CP-aligned probe trajectories confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point (\cref{fig:trajectory}).\looseness=-1

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Temporal window effect on R1-8B HS probe (L14). Precision: 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR: 9.65\% to 4.83\%.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{CP-aligned probe score trajectories. Each panel averages all test traces for one model, with step 0 at the true commitment point. HS probes (blue, solid) show a sharper score increase at CP than text probes (orange, dashed), with OT-7B showing the strongest separation and R1-32B the weakest, consistent with the evidence gradient. Shaded bands: $\pm$1 std. Points with $<$5 traces are omitted.}
\label{fig:trajectory}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\% detection on 3/4 models); Mahalanobis yields near-zero separation; RepE~\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\ 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns temporally; cross-model transfer fails (\cref{app:negative_details}). Per-model failure analysis (\cref{app:negative_details}) identifies mechanism-specific causes: BOCPD sensitivity to $\lambda$ prior, Mahalanobis collapse under high-dimensional HS, and CCS temporal misalignment. The signal is directional, gradual, model-specific, and linearly separable, excluding change-point, distance, transfer, and objective-alignment methods.\looseness=-1

